        "heading": "G EXTENSION: REWARD-FREE EXPLORATION",
        "text": "The problem of reward-free exploration investigates the problem where 1) the learning agent interacts with the environment initially without the reward information to gather enough environmental information 2) so that the subsequent offline phase could learn near-optimal policies of a wide range of choices of reward functions via the pre-gathered information (e.g., Jin et al., 2020a; Zhang et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2020a; Zanette et al., 2020b; Chen et al., 2022).\nThis section provides a reward-free extension of the results above. We introduce another function class G, which is only used for reward-free exploration phase. The original function classF is used with target rewardR (unknown during exploration) for the offline phase. We first definePh to be the zero-reward Bellman operator for horizon h\u2208 [H]. That is, for any gh\u2208Gh and any h\u2208 [H],\n(Phgh+1)(xh,ah) := \u2211 x\u2032 Ph(xh+1|xh,ah) max ah+1\u2208A gh+1(xh+1,ah+1).\nAssumption 2 (Reward-free completeness). Let T1:H be the Bellman operator with the target reward functionR, andF be the function class used to optimize the target reward function. Then for all h\u2208 [H] (a) PhGh+1\u2208Gh for all gh+1\u2208Gh+1 (b) Fh\u2212ThFh+1\u2286Gh\u2212PhGh+1.\nSimilar to Assumption 1, Assumption 2(a) is assumped to control the squared Bellman error with zero reward. Assumption 2(b) is made to guarantee the test function class in reward-free exploration phase (Gh\u2212PhGh+1 for layer h\u2208 [H], see Algorithm 2) is richer than that of offline phase (Fh\u2212ThFh+1 for layer h\u2208 [H], see Algorithm 3). Without loss of generality, we assume |G|=max{|F|,|G|}, otherwise one can simply replace all |G|with |F| in the proof below without violate other parts. In this section, we also introduce the reward-free variant of self-normalized coefficient as follows, which just modify Definition 8 using the reward-free counterparts.\nDefinition 13 (Self-Normalized Coefficient for Reward-Free RL). For each h\u2208 [H], let D\u03a0Gh :={d\u03c0h : \u03c0\u2208\u03a0G} and Gh\u2212PhGh+1 :={gh\u2212Phgh+1 :g\u2208G}. Then we define,\nSNCRL,rf(G,\u03a0G ,T ) := max h\u2208[H] SNC(Gh\u2212PhGh+1,D\u03a0Gh ,T ).\nUnder the same arguments (and same proofs) as Section 5.2, the reward-free variant of self-normalized coefficient could also subsume the reward-free counterparts of Bellman-Eluder dimension and coverability, which we omit here.\nG.1 ALGORITHM AND THEORETICAL ANALYSIS\nAlgorithm 2 Reward-Free Exploration with GOLF Input: D(0)h,rf\u2190\u2205, \u2200h\u2208 [H]. Function class for reward-free exploration G. G(0)\u2190G.\n1: for episode t=1,2,...,T do 2: Select policy \u03c0(t)\u2190\u03c0g(t) , where g(t)=argmaxg\u2208G(t\u22121)g(s1,\u03c0g,1). 3: Execute \u03c0(t) for one episode and obtain { x(t)1 ,a (t) 1 ,x (t) 2 ,...,s (t) H ,a (t) H ,x (t) H+1 } . 4: Update historical dataD(t)h,rf\u2190D (t\u22121) h,rf \u22c3{( x(t)h ,a (t) h ,x (t) h+1 )} , \u2200h\u2208 [H]. 5: Compute confidence set:\nG(t)\u2190 { g\u2208G :L(t)h,rf(gh,gh+1)\u2212 min\ng\u2032h\u2208Gh L(t)h,rf(g \u2032 h,gh+1)\u2264\u03b2rf , \u2200h\u2208 [H]\n} , (30)\nwhere L(t)h,rf(g,g \u2032) := \u2211 (x,a,x\u2032)\u2208D(t)h,rf\n[( g(x,a)\u2212max\na\u2032\u2208A g\u2032(x\u2032,a\u2032)\n)2] , \u2200g,g\u2032\u2208G.\n6: Select t\u22c6\u2190argmint\u2208[T ]g (t) 1 (s1,\u03c0 (t)). 7: Return dataD(t\u22c6\u22121)h,rf , \u2200h\u2208 [H].\nAlgorithm 3 Offline GOLF with Exploration Data and Target Reward Input: Exploration data from Algorithm 2, denoted byDh,rf , \u2200h\u2208 [H]. Target reward function, R. Function class for offline RL,F .\n1: Compute confidence set: F (off)\u2190 { f \u2208F :L(off)h (fh,fh+1)\u2212 min\nf \u2032h\u2208Fh L(off)h (f \u2032 h,fh+1)\u2264\u03b2off , \u2200h\u2208 [H]\n} , (31)\nwhere L(off)h (f,f \u2032) := \u2211 (x,a,x\u2032)\u2208Dh,rf\n[( f(x,a)\u2212R(x,a)\u2212max\na\u2032\u2208A f \u2032(x\u2032,a\u2032)\n)2] , \u2200f,f \u2032\u2208F .\n2: Return \u03c0\u0302\u2190\u03c0f\u0302 , where f\u0302=argmaxf\u2208F(off)f(s1,\u03c0f ).\nRecall that the key ideas in GOLF are: 1) using optimism to transit regret to on-policy average Bellman error; 2) using squared Bellman error to construct version space, which is the foundation of optimism. In the reward-free case, these two ideas can be exactly reproduced via the same algorithm as GOLF but with zero rewards (Algorithm 2). However, it is unclear whether running some simple offline RL algorithm on the exploration data of GOLF could guarantee to find a near-optimal policy, especially given the PAC guarantee of GOLF relies on outputting a uniform mixture of all historical policies (see, e.g., Corollary 2).\nTo address such issues, one can imagine that, if we know which is the best over all historical policies (say, \u03c0(t\u22c6) for some t\u22c6), could running one-step GOLF on the exploration data at t\u22c6 (Algorithm 3) guarantee to find a good policy? Note that, for the original GOLF algorithm (in the known-reward case), running so directly reproduces \u03c0(t\u22c6). Although knowing which is the best over all historical policies seems impossible in the known-reward case, thanks to the reward-free nature, we will show that the\nvalue of g(s1,\u03c0g,1) directly captures \u201chow bad is g\u201d (akin to the regret in the known-reward case), which allow us to find the best step over the reward-free exploration phase.\nIn the following contents, we formally prove that such algorithmic ideas could solve the reward-free RL problem. Theorem 22. Under Assumptions 1 and 2, there exists an absolute constants c1 and c2 such that for any \u03b4 \u2208 (0,1] and T \u2208N+, if we choose \u03b2off = c1 \u00b7 log(TH|G|/\u03b4) and \u03b2rf = (c1+c2) \u00b7 log(TH|G|/\u03b4) in Algorithms 2 and 3, then with probability at least 1\u2212\u03b4, the policy \u03c0\u0302 output by Algorithm 3 has\nJ(\u03c0\u22c6)\u2212J(\u03c0\u0302)\u2264O ( H \u221a SNCRL,rf(G,\u03a0G ,T )log(TH|G|/\u03b4)\nT\n) .\nWe defer the proofs to Appendix G.2. We also introduce the following two lemmas, which are key to adapting the known-reward results to the reward-free case. Lemma 23 (Reward-free exploration overestimates regret). For any f \u2208 F , let g be defined as gh = fh\u2212Thfh+1+Phgh+1, \u2200h \u2208 [H]. Then for any (x,a,h) \u2208 X \u00d7A\u00d7 [H], we have gh(x,a)\u2265 fh(x,a)\u2212Q \u03c0f h (x,a).\nSince the Q-function for all policies in the zero-reward case are zero, Lemma 23 guarantees that, regret in the reward-free exploration phase\u2014(g1(x1,\u03c0g,1)\u22120) always upper bounds its counterpart of the offline phase\u2014(f1(x1,\u03c0f,1)\u2212Q \u03c0f h (x,\u03c0f,1)). Equipped with the optimism argument, we can show that if g1(x1,\u03c0g,1) is small, its corresponding \u03c0f (the f with fh\u2212Thfh+1=gh\u2212Phgh+1, \u2200h\u2208 [H]) also has small regret. Lemma 24 (Reward-free exploration has larger confidence set). Suppose Assumption 2 holds and under the same conditions as Theorem 22. For any f \u2208F (off) (defined in Eq. (31)), there must exist g\u2208G(t\u22c6\u22121) (defined in Eq. (30)), such that fh\u2212Thfh+1=gh\u2212Phgh+1, \u2200h\u2208 [H].\nLemma 24 ensures that the reward-free version space G(t\u22c6\u22121) subsumes the offline version spaceF (off). Thus, we can use the metrics during reward-free exploration to upper bound that of the offline phase.\nNote that, similar to what we mentioned in the known-reward case, our reward-free results can also be extended to the V-type without major change. See Appendix F.3 for details.\nComparison with Chen et al. (2022). We now discuss the connection to the closest related work on reward-free exploration for non-linear RL (Chen et al., 2022). This part only talks about the Q-type results of Chen et al. (2022). Similar arguments also likely apply to the V-type, but the detailed discussion about the V-type is beyond the scope of this paper.\n\u2022 Chen et al. (2022) ext